      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ah ok. https://tour.golang.org/methods/12 may help.\n\nSay you have:\nstruct fred {t bool}\nfunc (f *fred) foo() {\n  if f.t fmt.Printf(\"True\\n\")\n}\n\nvar xyz *fred \u003d nil\nvar abc interface{foo()} \u003d xyz\nif abc !\u003d nil {\n  abc.foo()\n}\n\n\nThe code will panic in *fred.foo() because the check abc !\u003d nil will pass because abc is not nil it is {*fred, nil} but when foo() is called the receiver f is nil.\n\nThere was some code that assigned *bpPropertySet to a variable of type interface{} and then tried to compare that variable with nil. The fix is to assign an untyped nil, i.e. just a literal nil itself to the variable. e.g.\n\nvar xyz *fred \u003d nil\n\nvar abc interface{foo()}\nif xyz \u003d\u003d nil {\n  abc \u003d nil\n} else {\n  abc \u003d xyz\n}\n\nif abc !\u003d nil {\n  abc.foo()\n}\n\nThe above code will not panic as abc is nil so will not call *fred.foo().",
